VeriSilicon VIPLite kernel driver 2.0.3 ("vipcore") for the Allwinner A733
NPU (VIP9000).

Source: https://github.com/radxa/allwinner-bsp
Commit: c83016d2e725d42430048487e7c1d910b40ccaf3
Path:   drivers/npu/aw_nna_vip/vip2 (sha256 of the unmodified files in
        UPSTREAM.sha256)
License: dual MIT / GPL per file (MODULE_LICENSE "Dual MIT/GPL").

Changes:
  - vendor Kbuild replaced by Makefile (same objects and defines for a
    platform device without the vendor IOMMU)
  - os/linux/platform/allwinner/vip_drv_device_platform.[ch] and
    vip_drv_device_ion.[ch] removed: they need the vendor kernel's sunxi-sid,
    sunxi-smc, devfreq-cooling and ION headers. platform_a733.c (new,
    GPL-2.0-only) replaces them: clocks, resets, runtime PM on the PCK-600
    NPU domain, fixed module clock rate from the node's clock-frequency;
    module parameter alt_rate (test only) takes the module clock from an
    existing mux parent instead of pll-npu.
    A rate above 492 MHz (852 MHz only) is used when the efuse speed bin
    (nvmem cell "dvfs", SID 0x4c) lists it at the rail's voltage; the bin
    table is the vendor's vf_mapping_table and npu-opp-table (SPEED.md)
  - platform_a733.c clears SYS_CFG+0x8 bit 1 at probe (module parameter
    sram_to_npu, default 1) to route the shared SRAM to the NPU, as the
    vendor U-Boot and radxa/allwinner-bsp 57c4003 (cubie-aiot-v1.4.8,
    not on the pinned cubie-aiot-v1.5.0 commit) do
  - mainline-compat.h (new): nth_page()
  - os/linux/vip_drv_device_driver.c: platform remove callback returns void
  - os/linux/allocator/vip_drv_mem_allocator_common.c: MODULE_IMPORT_NS takes
    a string
  - os/linux/allocator/vip_drv_mem_allocator_common.c: vipdrv_map_user(),
    vipdrv_map_user_dma_map() and vipdrv_map_user_dma_buf() set up the VMA
    vm_mmap() returned (vm_flags_set, vm_page_prot, remap_pfn_range /
    dma_mmap_coherent / dma_buf_mmap) under mmap_write_lock() instead of
    down_read() (or no lock): with per-VMA locks vm_flags_set() asserts the
    mmap write lock and warned on every NPU buffer map. The VMA must still
    cover the whole range, and the mapping is vm_munmap()ed when setup fails
    (it leaked). os/linux/vip_drv_device_driver.h: vipdrv_mmap_write_lock()
  - os/linux/allocator/vip_drv_mem_allocator_wrap_user_memory.c: read the
    VMA's flags and end before dropping mmap_lock
  - os/linux/allocator/vip_drv_mem_allocator_common.c: vipdrv_import_pfn_map()
    (wrapping a VM_PFNMAP mapping, e.g. another driver's dma-buf) resolves
    each page with follow_pfnmap_start()/follow_pfnmap_end() under
    mmap_read_lock(), faulting in an unpopulated PTE (or a read-only one in
    a writable VMA) once with fixup_user_fault(). From 6.6 the vendor compiled the PTE lookup out (the
    pte_offset_map_lock() module API went away in 6.5) and handed the NPU
    uninitialised PFNs; a lookup failure also returned VIP_SUCCESS. Every
    failure, a PFN without a struct page, and kernels 6.6 - 6.11 now return
    an error. vipdrv_import_page_map() pins with FOLL_LONGTERM, unpins when
    the cache setup fails and when pin_user_pages() returns a negative
    errno (it walked pages[] up to the errno cast to unsigned).
  - os/linux/allocator/vip_drv_mem_allocator_wrap_user_memory.c: a wrap
    range that starts in, or crosses, a hole between VMAs is refused
  - NPU write access follows what the kernel grants the caller (the vendor
    mapped every wrapped buffer NPU-writable unless userspace passed
    NPU_READ_ONLY, so a read-only file mapping could be DMA-written).
    os/linux/allocator/vip_drv_mem_allocator_wrap_user_memory.c: an
    NPU-writable wrap needs VM_WRITE on every VMA of the range;
    vipdrv_import_page_map() pins with FOLL_WRITE only then, and GUP
    refuses that pin on a VMA that is not VM_WRITE; vipdrv_import_pfn_map()
    walks with write set from the NPU access, so it needs VM_WRITE and a
    writable PTE. os/linux/allocator/vip_drv_mem_allocator_wrap_dma_buf.c:
    an NPU-writable wrap needs a dma-buf exported writable (FMODE_WRITE),
    a read-only one is mapped DMA_TO_DEVICE and its MAP_USER mapping is
    PROT_READ without VM_MAYWRITE (vipdrv_map_user_access()); the
    dma_buf_get() reference is kept instead of dropped and re-taken, an
    ERR_PTR from dma_buf_attach() is caught, and the attachment is mapped
    with the _unlocked calls. memory/vip_drv_video_memory.c: the user
    memory, dma-buf and physical wraps set the NPU MMU writable bit only
    for an NPU-writable wrap (the dma-buf and physical wraps ignored
    NPU_READ_ONLY)
  - os/linux/allocator/vip_drv_mem_allocator_common.c: the page-list flush
    handle records its DMA direction. A buffer the NPU only reads is
    mapped DMA_TO_DEVICE and its CPU cache is never invalidated (no
    FROM_DEVICE sync), so a cache invalidate cannot drop other writers'
    dirty lines of a page the process may only read.
    flush_cache_destroy_pages() unmaps with the direction it mapped with
    (the vendor mapped BIDIRECTIONAL and unmapped FROM_DEVICE);
    flush_cache_init_pages() frees its handle when
    sg_alloc_table_from_pages() fails (it leaked)
  - os/linux/vip_drv_device_driver.c: VIPDRV_INTRFACE_WRAP_USER_PHYSICAL
    (vip_create_buffer_from_physical: NPU and MAP_USER access to any
    physical address) needs CAP_SYS_RAWIO and an argument of exactly
    sizeof(vipdrv_wrap_user_physical_t) (it is not in vipdrv_command_data,
    so a short buffer was read and written past its end); physical_num is
    bounded by MAX_WRAP_USER_PHYSICAL_TABLE_SIZE. /dev/vipcore is created
    0660 (was 0666); cubie-a7a-npu's udev rule gives it group vipcore
  - VIPCORE_CAPTURE=1 builds only (CAPTURE.md; the packaged module is
    built without it and is unchanged): os/linux/vip_drv_capture.[ch]
    (new, GPL-2.0-only), the debugfs command-stream capture;
    memory/vip_drv_video_memory.[ch]: vipdrv_mem_capture_walk();
    task/vip_drv_task_common.c: capture hooks before the trigger and at
    the end of vipdrv_task_wait(); os/linux/vip_drv_device_driver.c:
    creates and removes the debugfs directory; Makefile: VIPCORE_CAPTURE.
    capture/: the board-side runner and the splitter (GPL-2.0-only)
